            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Case Scene: Campaign Manager Zhou's Chaotic Monday</h2>
<p>At 9 AM Monday, Zhou, campaign manager at a tool app going global, was bombarded with DingTalk messages:</p>
<ul>
<li>Southeast Asia account balance critical, need to spend $8,000 today</li>
<li>European new account still under review, creatives ready</li>
<li>US market lost an account last week, need urgent appeal</li>
<li>Finance demanding reconciliation of last week's top-ups vs spend</li>
</ul>
<p>Zhou rubbed her temples—she manages 7 Meta accounts, 4 TikTok accounts across 3 product lines and 5 markets. Account status varies wildly, optimizers work in silos, and the boss keeps asking "Why did our ad spend increase 15% last month?"</p>
<p>This isn't just Zhou's problem. <strong>The core contradiction in multi-account teams is: account count grows fast, but management capability doesn't upgrade accordingly.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>Problem Diagnosis: Four Typical Symptoms</h2>
<p>We categorize common multi-account issues into four types. Use this checklist for quick self-assessment:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Symptom</th>
<th>Manifestation</th>
<th>Severity</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Account blackout</td>
<td>Zero balance, payment failure, banned without notice</td>
<td>⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Resource misallocation</td>
<td>High-performing accounts over-invested, cold accounts ignored</td>
<td>⭐⭐⭐⭐</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Responsibility ambiguity</td>
<td>Optimizers deflecting, "Whose account is this?"</td>
<td>⭐⭐⭐</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Financial chaos</td>
<td>Top-ups, spend, refunds don't reconcile</td>
<td>⭐⭐⭐</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>For Zhou's team, <strong>account blackout</strong> was most critical. Last month, the Southeast Asia account hit zero, causing a 6-hour delivery interruption and losing approximately 1,200 potential users.</p>
<hr />
<h2>ABC Tiering Model: Every Account in Its Place</h2>
<p>Account tiering is essentially <strong>differentiated resource allocation</strong>. Not every account deserves equal attention.</p>
<h3>Class A Accounts: Core Assets, Priority Protection</h3>
<p><strong>Criteria (any one qualifies):</strong><br />
- Daily spend exceeds $5,000<br />
- Generates 80%+ of main product line's acquisitions<br />
- 30-day ROAS exceeds team average by 20%+ </p>
<p><strong>Actions:</strong><br />
1. Set independent balance alerts at "daily spend × 1.5"<br />
2. Check account status twice daily (10 AM, 4 PM)<br />
3. Maintain prepaid balance for minimum 3 days of spend<br />
4. Assign to senior optimizers exclusively</p>
<h3>Class B Accounts: Growth Engines, Steady Scaling</h3>
<p><strong>Criteria:</strong><br />
- Daily spend $1,000-5,000<br />
- In testing or ramp-up phase with clear ROI targets<br />
- Recent stable performance with scaling potential</p>
<p><strong>Actions:</strong><br />
1. Alert threshold at "daily spend × 2"<br />
2. Check account status daily<br />
3. Weekly top-up planning, maintain 5+ days prepaid<br />
4. Rotating optimizer assignment with clear handoff records</p>
<h3>Class C Accounts: Test Beds, Flexible Deployment</h3>
<p><strong>Criteria:</strong><br />
- Daily spend below $1,000<br />
- New account testing creatives or markets<br />
- Poor performance, under pause observation</p>
<p><strong>Actions:</strong><br />
1. Alert thresholds can be looser, use batch management<br />
2. Check every 2-3 days<br />
3. "Small amounts, frequent top-ups" strategy to control risk<br />
4. Can be managed by junior optimizers with weekly senior review</p>
<hr />
<h2>Zhou's Team Transformation: First Week After Tiering</h2>
<p>Two weeks after implementing account tiering, Zhou's team improved significantly:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Response Speed</strong>: Account issues shifted from "only know when problems occur" to "alert before problems happen." The Southeast Asia account caught low balance 4 hours early via alert, avoiding delivery interruption.</p>
<p><strong>Resource Optimization</strong>: Previously all 3 optimizers dealt with balance issues. After tiering: Class A 60% focus, Class B 30%, Class C 10%.</p>
<p><strong>Reconciliation Efficiency</strong>: Clear account categorization reduced weekly reconciliation from 2 hours of overtime to 30 minutes.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The most crucial change was <strong>mindset</strong>. Optimizers stopped feeling "every account is urgent" and began handling priorities rhythmically.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Action Checklist: Three Steps to Implementation</h2>
<p>If your team is ready to implement account tiering:</p>
<p><strong>Step 1: Account Inventory (1-2 days)</strong><br />
- List all ad accounts: platform, account name, product line<br />
- Calculate 30-day spend, ROI, account status<br />
- Note responsible person for each account</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Tier Assignment (0.5 day)</strong><br />
- Classify each account per ABC criteria<br />
- Align with team to ensure shared understanding<br />
- Create account tier table, publish to shared docs</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Process Solidification (3-5 days)</strong><br />
- Configure alert rules for each tier<br />
- Define check frequency per tier<br />
- Set top-up approval workflows<br />
- Establish weekly tier review mechanism</p>
<hr />
<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>Account tiering isn't a "set it and forget it" project. As business evolves, account status changes dynamically. <strong>Re-evaluate tiers monthly</strong> based on spend, ROI, and strategic priorities to adjust assignments.</p>
<p>Management essence is <strong>directing resources to where they're needed most</strong>. When every optimizer knows "how much attention this account deserves," team efficiency improvement becomes natural.</p>
